From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm: TF_GLM_OVN_SOLO_TO_START, a solo prompt step may end at a request start

With TF_GLM_REQUEST_STARTS=1 the server's chunk plan lists a start at every grid step and at each message start, and a
solo step (TF_GLM_CHUNK_SOLO=4096) was refused wherever a start lay inside its second chunk. A chat prompt ends in a
message start a few rows before its last row (the reply's header), so the last stretch of every such prompt longer
than one grid step ran as two passes where the rows before it ran as one: an 8,183-row prompt as 4,096 + 2,048 + 2,036
+ 3 rows. A warm turn that adds more than one grid step is cut the same way: 2,048 rows, the rest, then the reply's 3.
On three GB10s at TP=3 an nsys capture read the two short passes of that prompt at 1,903.5 ms where one pass of their
4,084 rows takes about 1,694 ms (the warmed 4,096-row passes of the same boot run at 414.7 us a row), 5.5% of that
request's first token; the capture's request read 0.75% over an un-profiled boot's a row. The switch is there so one
image can measure the two schedules against each other.

A solo step is two chunks of the cold plan run as one pass. cuda_prompt_cuts.soloRows states that directly: the
plan's next chunk must end at start + 2,048 (a grid step, no start inside it) and the step ends where the plan's
chunk after it ends (nextCut of that point: the next start, a grid step on, or the prompt's end). Without the switch
it returns null unless that end is the whole step's (min(len - start, 4,096)), which is exactly the rule before
(cutInside over a whole step; a host test pins the two against each other). With TF_GLM_OVN_SOLO_TO_START=1 the step
ends at the start instead: 4,096 + 4,084 + 3 rows for the prompt above. stepRows (every rank's size of a step from
its part), leaderRows (rank 0's) and prompt_solo.at (rank 0's choice) all go through soloRows with the same switch, so
the ranks size every step alike; a rank without the switch refuses the shorter part with error.BadChunk.
prompt_solo.takesTwo now reads the step's own end, so a second chunk of one row is still not taken, and a keep mark at
the middle grid point still refuses the step. prompt_solo.allowed (every rank's check) is unchanged: a step that ends
at a start has none inside it. leaderRows returns error.BadChunk for a wide step soloRows does not name, where it
returned 4,096 rows whatever the plan.

The pass itself is unchanged: prefillChunk runs the step's rows as one pass whatever their count, as it already does
for a solo step that ends at the prompt's end, and a state is kept when the next step starts at a marked row, which
the start still is. Replies are CHUNK_SOLO's: the tokens two chunks a step give. Default off: with the switch unset no
step is sized differently.

Host tests: soloRows, stepRows and leaderRows with and without the switch; the rule before against soloRows without
it on every cold cut of the test prompts; walks that size each step as rank 0 does and check it as every rank does
(every step starts on a cold cut, a solo step is exactly the plan's next two chunks, prompt_solo.allowed takes it);
the 8,183-row prompt's steps as measured (4 without, 3 with) and a prompt whose last stretch is under a grid step
(the same either way); prompt_solo.at and allowed on an engine fixture with the switch on and off, and from a row
where the index rings hold a step to the start and not a whole one. The registry row and the docs lines follow
TF_GLM_OVN_PIECE_KDA's. The engine logs the switch at boot.

GPU check: tf-cuda-test glm-solo-check (every rank, a real checkpoint) gains two prompts with request starts: the
8,183-row chat shape above, and one of 8,692 rows whose starts are off the 4-row index pool's edge (301, 2,349, 3,350
and 5,398), where with the switch a solo step runs from row 301 to the start at 3,350. Each is run a chunk a step and
solo where the rules allow, and the two must agree in the first token, the next window's logits and tokens, the
rings' bases and every state byte, as the check's other prompts must. The solo passes each takes are listed in the
check for both settings (1 without the switch, 2 with it), and the PASS line names the setting.

New here, not a re-seat: written on this stack at v1.2.9, on top of this package's 0001.

